{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent level bias: forecasts are generally too low by ~+1 to +3 €/t during rising regimes; apply an upward level shift when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend (positive slope).",
    "Underreaction to upside momentum: in strong rallies the model lags the move (misses the step-up for ~1–2 weeks). Increase near-term (days 1–15) drift toward the recent trend/last price when short-term momentum is positive.",
    "Over-mean-reversion at longer horizons: beyond ~day 20 the model tends to pull back too hard (predicts declines that don’t materialize). Damp mean-reversion strength for days 20–30, keeping the path closer to the prevailing level.",
    "Post-spike downside overshoot: after a sharp rise, the model often forecasts a deeper retracement than realized (notably in examples 4–5 late horizon). Cap drawdowns vs last observed price (e.g., limit projected drop to a fraction of recent 10-day realized volatility).",
    "Volatility/peak underestimation: local highs are systematically underpredicted in rally periods. Add a convexity/volatility uplift to peaks when recent realized volatility is elevated and momentum is positive."
  ]
}
